import * as THREE from 'three';
const loader = new THREE.TextureLoader();
const tex = loader.load('/textures/wood/diffuse.jpg');
const mat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ map: tex });
Loading is asynchronous, but the texture object is returned synchronously — the renderer simply skips it until pixels arrive. To know when ready:
loader.loadAsync('/textures/wood/diffuse.jpg').then((tex) => { /* ... */ });
In R3F (Post 12), useLoader + Suspense wraps this pattern.

const colorMap = loader.load('/diffuse.jpg');
colorMap.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace; // colour textures
const normalMap = loader.load('/normal.png');
normalMap.colorSpace = THREE.NoColorSpace; // data textures (default)
const roughnessMap = loader.load('/rough.png');
roughnessMap.colorSpace = THREE.NoColorSpace;
GLTFLoader sets colour spaces correctly for you. Hand-loaded TextureLoader does not — you must set them.
Renderer side:
renderer.outputColorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace; // default since r152
This converts the linear render back to sRGB for the monitor.
Trick: if your scene looks washed-out (light grey instead of vibrant), the first suspect is colour space. Set colorSpace = SRGBColorSpace on every colour texture and the output back to sRGB. The fix is one line per file.

Roughness, metalness, and AO are each single-channel. Packing them into one RGB image saves a texture slot:
R = AO
G = Roughness
B = Metalness
This is the glTF 2.0 convention. Both roughnessMap and metalnessMap can point at the same texture; Three.js reads the right channel.
const orm = loader.load('/wood/orm.jpg'); // ao + rough + metal
orm.colorSpace = THREE.NoColorSpace;
mat.aoMap = orm;
mat.roughnessMap = orm;
mat.metalnessMap = orm;
Trick: glTF authoring tools (Substance Painter, Blender, Mixer) export ORM packed by default. Save a draw call by packing.

A normal map encodes a surface normal per pixel as RGB. The shader fakes high-frequency detail without geometry.
const normal = loader.load('/normal.png');
normal.colorSpace = THREE.NoColorSpace;
mat.normalMap = normal;
mat.normalScale.set(1.0, 1.0); // y can be negated for some authoring tools

import { RGBELoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/RGBELoader.js';
new RGBELoader().load('/env/studio.hdr', (tex) => {
tex.mapping = THREE.EquirectangularReflectionMapping;
scene.environment = tex; // applied to every PBR material in scene
scene.background = tex; // optional — also render as the sky
});
scene.environment is the modern way — every PBR material picks it up automatically. No more setting mat.envMap on every material individually.

Sharp HDRs are aliased when used directly. The PMREM (Pre-filtered Mipmapped Radiance Environment) generator pre-blurs the env map at multiple roughness levels:
const pmrem = new THREE.PMREMGenerator(renderer);
const envMap = pmrem.fromEquirectangular(hdr).texture;
hdr.dispose();
pmrem.dispose();
scene.environment = envMap;
GLTFLoader handles PMREM automatically when a glTF has an embedded env. For hand-loaded HDRs, do it explicitly.

import { KTX2Loader } from 'three/addons/loaders/KTX2Loader.js';
const ktx2 = new KTX2Loader()
.setTranscoderPath('https://unpkg.com/three@0.184.0/examples/jsm/libs/basis/')
.detectSupport(renderer);
const tex = await ktx2.loadAsync('/textures/wood/diffuse.ktx2');
GLTFLoader chains in KTX2Loader automatically when a glTF references KTX2 textures:
gltfLoader.setKTX2Loader(ktx2);
